  "step_2": "The individual in the verbal report states that in A, the winning amount is 1% less likely (i.e., 33% vs. 34%) and the prize is slightly higher (2500 vs. 2400 Euros). However, the individual prioritizes winning a prize over the size of the prize. The individual concludes that because the probability of winning is higher in B (34% vs. 33%), they chose B. The justification is based on the higher probability of obtaining a prize, not on skewness or distribution shape. The individual does not mention anything about the probability of 0 Euros, nor do they discuss the shape of the distribution, skewness, or any comparison between unfavorable and favorable outcomes in terms of probability mass. The focus is solely on the probability of winning (i.e., avoiding the 0 Euro outcome), and the individual emphasizes the importance of obtaining a prize over the prize amount. Thus, the considered information is: (1) A has a slightly lower probability of winning (33% vs. 34%), (2) A has a slightly higher prize, (3) the individual values the chance of winning more than the prize size, and (4) they chose B because of the higher winning probability. No reference is made to skewness or the distribution of probabilities across outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness as defined — preferring the lottery with a greater portion of probability on unfavorable outcomes (i.e., 0 Euros). In this case, A has 67% on 0 Euros, B has 66%, so A is more right-skewed. The reason would thus prefer A. However, the individual’s deliberation focuses entirely on the probability of winning (i.e., the positive outcome), not on the probability of zero. The individual does not mention the 0 Euro outcomes or their distribution. There is no reference to skewness, right-skew, or any comparison of the mass of probability on unfavorable vs. favorable outcomes. The justification is based on a preference for higher probability of winning, not on distributional skewness. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason as defined. The information considered (winning probability) is different from the information required by the reason (distribution of probability across outcomes, especially on the unfavorable side). The justification (higher chance of winning) does not match the rule of the reason (prefer more right-skewed, i.e., more probability on 0 Euros). Hence, the reason was not used.",
